## an3.51
an3.51:1.1 | S: Then two old brahmins—elderly and senior, who were advanced in years and had reached the final stage of life, a hundred and twenty years old—went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. Then two brahmans—feeble old men, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life, 120 years old—went to the Blessed One. On arrival, they exchanged courteous greetings with him and,
an3.51:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, they sat down to one side, and said to the Buddha: | T: after an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, sat to one side. As they were sitting there, they said to him:
an3.51:1.3 | S: “We brahmins, worthy Gotama, are old, elderly and senior, we’re advanced in years and have reached the final stage of life; we’re a hundred and twenty years old. | T: “Master Gotama, we are brahmans—feeble old men, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life, 120 years old.
an3.51:1.4 | S: And we haven’t done what is good and skillful, nor have we made a shelter from fear. | T: And we have done no admirable deeds, no skillful deeds, no deeds that allay our fears.
an3.51:1.5 | S: Advise us, worthy Gotama, instruct us! It will be for our lasting welfare and happiness.” | T: Teach us, Master Gotama. Instruct us, Master Gotama, for our long-term benefit & happiness.”
an3.51:2.1 | S: “Indeed, brahmins, you’re old, elderly and senior. | T: “Indeed, brahmans, you are feeble old men, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life, 120 years old.
an3.51:2.2 | S: And you haven’t done what is good and skillful, nor have you made a shelter from fear. | T: And you have done no admirable deeds, no skillful deeds, no deeds that allay your fears.
an3.51:2.3 | S: This world is led onward by old age, sickness, and death. | T: This world is swept away by aging, by illness, by death. With the world thus swept away by aging, illness, & death,
an3.51:2.4 | S: But restraint here by way of body, speech, and mind is the shelter, asylum, island, refuge, and final haven for the departed. | T: any restraint of body, speech, & intellect practiced here will be one’s shelter, cave, island, & refuge after death in the world beyond.”
an3.51:3.1 | S: This life, so very short, is led onward. | T: It’s swept along: life, its next-to-nothing span.
an3.51:3.2 | S: One led on to old age has no shelter. | T: For one swept on by aging no shelters exist.
an3.51:3.3 | S: Seeing this peril in death, | T: Keeping sight of this danger in death,
an3.51:3.4 | S: do good deeds that bring you joy. | T: do merit-deeds that bring bliss.
an3.51:4.1 | S: The restraint practiced here— | T: When you’re restrained here
an3.51:4.2 | S: of body, speech, and mind— | T: in body, speech, & awareness;
an3.51:4.3 | S: leads the departed to happiness, | T: (none)
an3.51:4.4 | S: as the good deeds done while living.” | T: when you make merit while still alive: That will be for your bliss after death.
an3.51:4.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.183
an4.183:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
an4.183:1.2 | S: Then Vassakāra the brahmin, a chief minister of Magadha, went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Vassakāra the brahman, the minister to the king of Magadha, approached the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an4.183:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an4.183:2.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama, this is my doctrine and view: | T: “I am of the view, of the opinion,
an4.183:2.2 | S: There’s nothing wrong with talking about what you’ve seen, saying: | T: that when anyone speaks of what he has seen, (saying,)
an4.183:2.3 | S: ‘So I have seen.’ | T: ‘Thus have I seen,’ there is no fault in that.
an4.183:2.4 | S: There’s nothing wrong with talking about what you’ve heard, saying: | T: When anyone speaks of what he has heard, (saying,)
an4.183:2.5 | S: ‘So I have heard.’ | T: ‘Thus have I heard,’ there is no fault in that.
an4.183:2.6 | S: There’s nothing wrong with talking about what you’ve thought, saying: | T: When anyone speaks of what he has sensed, (saying,)
an4.183:2.7 | S: ‘So I have thought.’ | T: ‘Thus have I sensed,’ there is no fault in that.
an4.183:2.8 | S: There’s nothing wrong with talking about what you’ve known, saying: | T: When anyone speaks of what he has cognized, (saying,)
an4.183:2.9 | S: ‘So I have known.’” | T: ‘Thus have I cognized,’ there is no fault in that.”
an4.183:3.1 | S: “Brahmin, I don’t say you should talk about everything you see, hear, think, and know. | T: (The Blessed One responded:) “I do not say, brahman, that everything that has been seen should be spoken about. Nor do I say that everything that has been seen should not be spoken about. I do not say that everything that has been heard… everything that has been sensed… everything that has been cognized should be spoken about. Nor do I say that everything that has been cognized should not be spoken about.
an4.183:3.2 | S: But I also don’t say you should talk about nothing you see, hear, think, and know. | T: (none)
an4.183:3.3 | P: nāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ sutaṁ bhāsitabbanti vadāmi; | T: (none)
an4.183:3.4 | P: na panāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ sutaṁ na bhāsitabbanti vadāmi; | T: (none)
an4.183:3.5 | P: nāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ mutaṁ bhāsitabbanti vadāmi; | T: (none)
an4.183:3.6 | P: na panāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ mutaṁ na bhāsitabbanti vadāmi; | T: (none)
an4.183:3.7 | P: nāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ viññātaṁ bhāsitabbanti vadāmi; | T: (none)
an4.183:3.8 | P: na panāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ viññātaṁ na bhāsitabbanti vadāmi. | T: (none)
an4.183:4.1 | S: When talking about certain things you’ve seen, heard, thought, or known, unskillful qualities grow while skillful qualities decline. I say that you shouldn’t talk about those things. | T: “When, for one who speaks of what has been seen, unskillful qualities increase and skillful qualities decrease, then that sort of thing should not be spoken about. But when, for one who speaks of what has been seen, unskillful qualities decrease and skillful qualities increase, then that sort of thing should be spoken about.
an4.183:4.2 | S: When talking about other things you’ve seen, heard, thought, or known, unskillful qualities decline while skillful qualities grow. I say that you should talk about those things.” | T: “When, for one who speaks of what has been heard… what has been sensed… what has been cognized, unskillful qualities increase and skillful qualities decrease, then that sort of thing should not be spoken about. But when, for one who speaks of what has been cognized, unskillful qualities decrease and skillful qualities increase, then that sort of thing should be spoken about.”
