## an4.36
an4.36:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was traveling along the road between Ukkaṭṭhā and Setavyā, | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was traveling along the road between Ukkaṭṭha and Setabya, and Doṇa the brahman was also traveling along the road between Ukkaṭṭha and Setabya.
an4.36:1.2 | S: as was the brahmin Doṇa. | T: (none)
an4.36:1.3 | S: Doṇa saw that the Buddha’s footprints had thousand-spoked wheels, with rims and hubs, complete in every detail. | T: Doṇa the brahman saw, in the Blessed One’s footprints, wheels with 1,000 spokes, together with rims and hubs, complete in all their features.
an4.36:1.4 | S: It occurred to him, | T: On seeing them, the thought occurred to him,
an4.36:1.5 | S: “Oh lord, how incredible, how amazing! | T: “How amazing! How astounding!
an4.36:1.6 | S: Surely these couldn’t be the footprints of a human being?” | T: These are not the footprints of a human being!”
an4.36:1.7 | S: The Buddha had left the road and sat at the root of a tree cross-legged, setting his body straight, and bringing mindfulness to the present. | T: Then the Blessed One, leaving the road, went to sit at the root of a certain tree—his legs crossed, his body erect, with mindfulness established to the fore.
an4.36:1.8 | S: Then Doṇa, following the Buddha’s footprints, saw him sitting at the tree root—impressive and inspiring, with peaceful faculties and heart, attained to the highest self-control and serenity, like an elephant with tamed, guarded, and controlled faculties. | T: Then Doṇa, following the Blessed One’s footprints, saw him sitting at the root of the tree: confident, inspiring confidence, his senses calmed, his mind calmed, having attained the utmost control & tranquility, tamed, guarded, his senses restrained, a nāga.
an4.36:1.9 | S: He went up to the Buddha and said to him: | T: On seeing him, he went to him and said,
an4.36:2.1 | S: “Sir, might you be a god?” | T: “Master, are you a deva?”
an4.36:2.2 | S: “I will not be a god, brahmin.” | T: “No, brahman, I am not a deva.”
an4.36:2.3 | S: “Might you be a centaur?” | T: “Are you a gandhabba?”
an4.36:2.4 | S: “I will not be a centaur.” | T: “No….”
an4.36:2.5 | S: “Might you be a spirit?” | T: “… a yakkha?”
an4.36:2.6 | S: “I will not be a spirit.” | T: “No….”
an4.36:2.7 | S: “Might you be a human?” | T: “… a human being?”
an4.36:2.8 | S: “I will not be a human.” | T: “No, brahman, I am not a human being.”
an4.36:3.1 | S: “When asked whether you might be a god, centaur, spirit, or human, | T: “When asked, ‘Are you a deva?’
an4.36:3.2 | S: you answer that you will not be any of these. | T: you answer, ‘No, brahman, I am not a deva.’
an4.36:3.3 | P: ‘Gandhabbo no bhavaṁ bhavissatī’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno: | T: When asked, ‘Are you a gandhabba?’
an4.36:3.4 | P: ‘na kho ahaṁ, brāhmaṇa, gandhabbo bhavissāmī’ti vadesi. | T: you answer, ‘No, brahman, I am not a gandhabba.’
an4.36:3.5 | P: ‘Yakkho no bhavaṁ bhavissatī’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno: | T: When asked, ‘Are you a yakkha?’
an4.36:3.6 | P: ‘na kho ahaṁ, brāhmaṇa, yakkho bhavissāmī’ti vadesi. | T: you answer, ‘No, brahman, I am not a yakkha.’
an4.36:3.7 | P: ‘Manusso no bhavaṁ bhavissatī’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno: | T: When asked, ‘Are you a human being?’
an4.36:3.8 | P: ‘na kho ahaṁ, brāhmaṇa, manusso bhavissāmī’ti vadesi. | T: you answer, ‘No, brahman, I am not a human being.’
an4.36:3.9 | S: So what then might you be?” | T: Then what sort of being are you?”
an4.36:4.1 | S: “Brahmin, if I had not given up defilements I might have become a god … a centaur … a spirit … or a human. But I have given up those defilements, cut them off at the root, made them like a palm stump, obliterated them so they are unable to arise in the future. | T: “Brahman, the effluents by which—if they were not abandoned—I would be a deva: Those are abandoned by me, their root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising.
