an4.36:4.3 | S: Suppose there was a blue water lily, or a pink or white lotus. Though it sprouted and grew in the water, it would rise up above the water and stand with no water clinging to it. | T: “Just like a red, blue, or white lotus—born in the water, grown in the water, rising up above the water—stands unsmeared by the water,
an4.36:4.4 | S: In the same way, though I was born and grew up in the world, I live having mastered the world, unsullied by the world. | T: in the same way I—born in the world, grown in the world, having overcome the world—live unsmeared by the world.
an4.36:4.5 | S: Remember me, brahmin, as a Buddha. | T: Remember me, brahman, as ‘awakened.’
an4.36:5.1 | S: I could have been reborn as a god, | T: “The effluents by which I would go to a deva-state,
an4.36:5.2 | S: or as a centaur flying through the sky. | T: or become a gandhabba in the sky,
an4.36:5.3 | S: I could have become a spirit, | T: or go to a yakkha-state & human-state:
an4.36:5.4 | S: or returned as a human. | T: (none)
an4.36:5.5 | S: But I’ve ended those defilements, | T: Those have been destroyed by me,
an4.36:5.6 | S: they’re blown away and mown down. | T: ruined, their stems removed.
an4.36:6.1 | S: Like a graceful lotus, | T: Like a blue lotus, rising up,
an4.36:6.2 | S: to which water does not cling, | T: unsmeared by water,
an4.36:6.3 | S: the world doesn’t cling to me, | T: unsmeared am I by the world,
an4.36:6.4 | S: and so, brahmin, I am a Buddha.” | T: and so, brahman, I’m awake.”
an4.36:6.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.94
an4.94:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these four individuals are found in the world. | T: “Monks, these four types of individuals are to be found existing in world.
an4.94:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.94:1.3 | S: One individual has internal serenity of heart, but not the higher wisdom of discernment of principles. | T: “There is the case of the individual who has attained internal tranquility of awareness, but not insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:1.4 | S: One individual has the higher wisdom of discernment of principles, but not internal serenity of heart. | T: Then there is the case of the individual who has attained insight into phenomena through heightened discernment, but not internal tranquility of awareness.
an4.94:1.5 | S: One individual has neither internal serenity of heart, nor the higher wisdom of discernment of principles. | T: Then there is the case of the individual who has attained neither internal tranquility of awareness nor insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:1.6 | S: One individual has both internal serenity of heart, and the higher wisdom of discernment of principles. | T: And then there is the case of the individual who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:2.1 | S: As for the individual who has serenity but not discernment: they should approach someone who has discernment and ask: | T: “The individual who has attained internal tranquility of awareness, but not insight into phenomena through heightened discernment, should approach an individual who has attained insight into phenomena through heightened discernment and ask him:
an4.94:2.2 | S: ‘Reverend, how should conditions be seen? | T: ‘How should fabrications be regarded? How should they be investigated? How should they be seen with insight?’
an4.94:2.3 | S: How should they be comprehended? | T: (none)
an4.94:2.4 | S: How should they be discerned?’ | T: (none)
an4.94:2.5 | S: They would answer from their own experience: | T: The other will answer in line with what he has seen & experienced:
an4.94:2.6 | S: ‘This is how conditions should be seen, comprehended, and discerned.’ | T: ‘Fabrications should be regarded in this way. Fabrications should be investigated in this way. Fabrications should be seen in this way with insight.’
an4.94:2.7 | S: After some time they have both serenity and discernment. | T: Then eventually he [the first] will become one who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:3.1 | S: As for the individual who has discernment but not serenity: they should approach someone who has serenity and ask: | T: “As for the individual who has attained insight into phenomena through heightened discernment, but not internal tranquility of awareness, he should approach an individual who has attained internal tranquility of awareness… and ask him:
an4.94:3.2 | S: ‘Reverend, how should the mind be stilled? | T: ‘How should the mind be steadied?
an4.94:3.3 | S: How should it be settled? | T: How should it be made to settle down?
an4.94:3.4 | S: How should it be unified? | T: How should it be unified?
an4.94:3.5 | S: How should it be immersed in samādhi?’ | T: How should it be concentrated?’
an4.94:3.6 | S: They would answer from their own experience: | T: The other will answer in line with what he has seen & experienced:
an4.94:3.7 | S: ‘Reverend, this is how the mind should be stilled, settled, unified, and immersed in samādhi.’ | T: ‘The mind should be steadied in this way. The mind should be made to settle down in this way. The mind should be unified in this way. The mind should be concentrated in this way.’
an4.94:3.8 | S: After some time they have both discernment and serenity. | T: Then eventually he [the first] will become one who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:4.1 | S: As for the individual who has neither serenity nor discernment: they should approach someone who has serenity and discernment and ask: | T: “As for the individual who has attained neither internal tranquility of awareness nor insight into phenomena through heightened discernment, he should approach an individual who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment… and ask him:
an4.94:4.2 | S: ‘Reverend, how should the mind be stilled? | T: ‘How should the mind be steadied?
an4.94:4.3 | S: How should it be settled? | T: How should it be made to settle down?
an4.94:4.4 | S: How should it be unified? | T: How should it be unified?
an4.94:4.5 | S: How should it be immersed in samādhi?’ | T: How should it be concentrated?
an4.94:4.6 | S: How should conditions be seen? | T: How should fabrications be regarded?
an4.94:4.7 | S: How should they be comprehended? | T: How should they be investigated?
an4.94:4.8 | S: How should they be discerned?’ | T: How should they be seen with insight?’
an4.94:4.9 | S: They would answer as they’ve seen and known: | T: The other will answer in line with what he has seen & experienced:
an4.94:4.10 | S: ‘Reverend, this is how the mind should be stilled, settled, unified, and immersed in samādhi. And this is how conditions should be seen, comprehended, and discerned.’ | T: ‘The mind should be steadied in this way. The mind should be made to settle down in this way. The mind should be unified in this way. The mind should be concentrated in this way. Fabrications should be regarded in this way. Fabrications should be investigated in this way. Fabrications should be seen in this way with insight.’
an4.94:4.11 | S: After some time they have both serenity and discernment. | T: Then eventually he [the first] will become one who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
