## snp1.1
snp1.1:1.1 | S: When anger surges, they drive it out, | T: The monk who subdues his arisen anger
snp1.1:1.2 | S: as with medicine a cobra’s spreading venom. | T: as, with herbs, snake-venom once it has spread,
snp1.1:1.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:1.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:2.1 | S: They’ve cut off greed entirely, | T: The monk who has cut off passion without leaving a trace,
snp1.1:2.2 | S: like a lotus plucked flower and stalk. | T: as he would, plunging into a lake, a lotus,
snp1.1:2.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:2.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:3.1 | S: They’ve cut off craving entirely, | T: The monk who has cut off craving without leaving a trace,
snp1.1:3.2 | S: drying up that swift-flowing stream. | T: drying up the swift-flowing flood,
snp1.1:3.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:3.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:4.1 | S: They’ve swept away conceit entirely, | T: The monk who has uprooted conceit without leaving a trace,
snp1.1:4.2 | S: as a fragile bridge of reeds by a great flood. | T: as a great flood, a very weak bridge made of reeds,
snp1.1:4.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:4.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:5.1 | S: In future lives they find no worth, | T: The monk seeing in states of becoming no essence,
snp1.1:5.2 | S: as an inspector of fig trees finds no flower. | T: as he would, when examining fig trees, no flowers,
snp1.1:5.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:5.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:6.1 | S: They hide no anger within, | T: The monk with no inner anger,
snp1.1:6.2 | S: passed beyond any kind of existence. | T: who has thus gone beyond becoming & not-,
snp1.1:6.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:6.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:7.1 | S: Their mental vibrations are cleared away, | T: The monk whose discursive thoughts are dispersed,
snp1.1:7.2 | S: internally clipped off entirely. | T: well-dealt with inside without leaving a trace,
snp1.1:7.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:7.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:8.1 | S: They have not run too far nor run back, | T: The monk who hasn’t slipped past or held back,
snp1.1:8.2 | S: but have gone beyond all this proliferation. | T: transcending all this objectification,
snp1.1:8.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:8.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:9.1 | S: They have not run too far nor run back, | T: The monk who hasn’t slipped past or held back,
snp1.1:9.2 | S: for they know that nothing in the world <j>is what it seems. | T: knowing with regard to the world that “All this is unreal,”
snp1.1:9.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:9.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
