snp1.1:12.1 | S: They have not run too far nor run back, | T: The monk who hasn’t slipped past or held back,
snp1.1:12.2 | S: knowing nothing is what it seems, free of hate. | T: without aversion, as “All this is unreal,”
snp1.1:12.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:12.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:13.1 | S: They have not run too far nor run back, | T: The monk who hasn’t slipped past or turned back,
snp1.1:13.2 | S: knowing nothing is what it seems, free of delusion. | T: without delusion, as “All this is unreal,”
snp1.1:13.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:13.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:14.1 | S: They have no underlying tendencies at all, | T: The monk in whom there are no obsessions
snp1.1:14.2 | S: and are rid of unskillful roots, | T: —the roots of unskillfulness totally destroyed—
snp1.1:14.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:14.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:15.1 | S: They have nothing born of distress at all | T: The monk in whom there’s nothing born of disturbance
snp1.1:15.2 | S: to make them to return to the near shore. | T: that would lead him back to this shore,
snp1.1:15.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:15.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:16.1 | S: No snarl grows in them at all, | T: The monk in whom there’s nothing born of the underbrush
snp1.1:16.2 | S: that would shackle them to a new life. | T: that would act as a cause for binding him to becoming,
snp1.1:16.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:16.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:17.1 | S: They’ve given up the five hindrances, | T: The monk who’s abandoned five hindrances,
snp1.1:17.2 | S: untroubled, rid of indecision, free of thorns. | T: who, untroubled, de-arrowed, has crossed over doubt,

