## ud2.7
ud2.7:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud2.7:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud2.7:1.3 | S: Now at that time a certain lay follower’s dear and beloved only child passed away. | T: And on that occasion the dear and beloved only son of a certain lay follower had died.
ud2.7:2.1 | S: Then in the middle of the day several lay followers with wet clothes and hair went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to them: | T: So a large number of lay followers–their clothes wet, their hair wet–went to the Blessed One in the middle of the day and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As they were sitting there the Blessed One said to them:
ud2.7:2.2 | S: “Why, lay followers, have you come here in the middle of the day with wet clothes and hair?” | T: “Why have you come here–your clothes wet, your hair wet–in the middle of the day?”
ud2.7:3.1 | S: The lay follower replied, | T: When this was said, the lay follower said to the Blessed One,
ud2.7:3.2 | S: “Sir, my dear and beloved only child has passed away. | T: “My dear and beloved only son, lord, has died.
ud2.7:3.3 | S: That’s why we came here in the middle of the day with wet clothes and hair.” | T: This is why we have come here–our clothes wet, our hair wet–in the middle of the day.”
ud2.7:4.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud2.7:5.1 | S: “Hosts of gods and most human beings are bound | T: Tied down by the allure of what seems dear, hosts of devas, most human beings,
ud2.7:5.2 | S: to what seems dear and pleasant. | T: (none)
ud2.7:5.3 | S: Gloomy and exhausted, | T: worn out with misery,
ud2.7:5.4 | S: they fall under the sway of the King of Death. | T: fall under the sway of the King of Death.
ud2.7:6.1 | S: The diligent, who day and night | T: But those who, day & night,
ud2.7:6.2 | S: give up what seems pleasant, | T: heedfully abandon what seems dear,
ud2.7:6.3 | S: dig out the root of gloom— | T: dig up misery by the root–
ud2.7:6.4 | S: Death’s bait so hard to escape.” | T: Death’s bait so hard to overcome.
ud2.7:6.5 | P: Sattamaṁ. | T: (none)

## ud5.4
ud5.4:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
ud5.4:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī at Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
ud5.4:1.3 | S: Now at that time, between Sāvatthī and the Jeta Grove, several boys were tormenting some fish. | T: And on that occasion, a large number of boys on the road between Sāvatthī & Jeta’s Grove were catching fish.
ud5.4:2.1 | S: Then the Buddha robed up in the morning and, taking his bowl and robe, entered Sāvatthī for alms. | T: Then early in the morning the Blessed One adjusted his under robe and–carrying his bowl & robes–went into Sāvatthī for alms.
ud5.4:2.2 | S: He saw the boys tormenting the fish. | T: He saw the large number of boys on the road between Sāvatthī & Jeta’s Grove catching little fish.
ud5.4:2.3 | S: He went to them and said, | T: Seeing them, he went up to them and, on arrival, said to them,
ud5.4:2.4 | S: “Boys, do you fear pain? Do you dislike pain?” | T: “Boys, do you fear pain? Do you dislike pain?”
ud5.4:2.5 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. “We dislike pain.” | T: “Yes, lord, we fear pain. We dislike pain.”
ud5.4:3.1 | S: Then, understanding this matter, on that occasion the Buddha expressed this heartfelt sentiment: | T: Then, on realizing the significance of that, the Blessed One on that occasion exclaimed:
ud5.4:4.1 | S: “If you fear pain, | T: If you fear pain,
ud5.4:4.2 | S: if you dislike pain, | T: if you dislike pain,
ud5.4:4.3 | S: don’t do bad deeds | T: don’t anywhere do an evil deed
ud5.4:4.4 | S: either openly or in secret. | T: in open or in secret.
ud5.4:5.1 | S: If you should do a bad deed, | T: If you’re doing or will do an evil deed,
ud5.4:5.2 | S: now or in the future, | T: (none)
ud5.4:5.3 | S: you won’t be freed from suffering, | T: you won’t escape pain
ud5.4:5.4 | S: though you fly away and flee.” | T: catching up as you run away.
ud5.4:5.5 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.229
sn35.229:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, an unlearned ordinary person speaks of the ocean. | T: “‘The ocean, the ocean,’ says the uninstructed run-of-the-mill person.
sn35.229:1.2 | S: But that’s not the ocean in the training of the Noble One. | T: But that’s not the ocean in the discipline of the noble ones.
sn35.229:1.3 | S: That’s just a large body of water, a large sea of water. | T: It’s a great mass of water, a great body of water.
sn35.229:1.4 | S: There are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are forms cognizable via the eye—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked with sensual desire.
sn35.229:1.5 | S: This is called the ocean in the training of the Noble One. | T: These are called the ocean in the discipline of the noble ones.
sn35.229:1.6 | S: And it’s here that this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, gods and humans—is for the most part sunk. It’s become tangled like string, knotted like a ball of thread, and matted like rushes and reeds, and it doesn’t escape the places of loss, the bad places, the underworld, transmigration. | T: Here this cosmos—with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk—is, for the most part, submerged, like a tangled skein, a knotted ball of string, like matted rushes & reeds, and doesn’t go beyond the plane of deprivation, the bad destination, the lower realm, the wandering-on.
sn35.229:2.1 | S: There are sounds … smells … tastes … touches … ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are sounds cognizable via the ear… aromas cognizable via the nose… flavors cognizable via the tongue… tactile sensations cognizable via the body… “There are ideas cognizable via the intellect—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked with sensual desire.
sn35.229:2.2 | S: This is called the ocean in the training of the Noble One. | T: These are called the ocean in the discipline of the noble ones.
sn35.229:2.3 | S: And it’s here that this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, gods and humans—is for the most part sunk. It’s become tangled like string, knotted like a ball of thread, and matted like rushes and reeds, and it doesn’t escape the places of loss, the bad places, the underworld, transmigration. | T: Here this cosmos—with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk—is, for the most part, submerged, like a tangled skein, a knotted ball of string, like matted rushes & reeds, and doesn’t go beyond the plane of deprivation, the bad destination, the lower realm, the wandering-on.
sn35.229:3.1 | S: Those in whom greed, hate, and ignorance | T: Whoever has become dispassionate to passion, aversion, & ignorance,
sn35.229:3.2 | S: have faded away; | T: (none)
sn35.229:3.3 | S: have crossed the ocean so hard to cross, | T: crosses over this ocean,
sn35.229:3.4 | S: with its saltwater crocodiles and monsters, <j>its waves and dangers. | T: with its danger of sharks, demons, waves, so very hard to cross.
sn35.229:4.1 | S: They’ve slipped their chains, given up death, and have no attachments. | T: He’s one who has overcome ties, forsaken death,
sn35.229:4.2 | S: They’ve given up suffering, <j>so there are no more future lives. | T: abandoned suffering & stress, is acquisition-free with no further becoming.
sn35.229:4.3 | S: They’ve disappeared, and cannot be measured; | T: Having gone to his end, he has no measure,
sn35.229:4.4 | S: and they’ve confounded the King of Death, I say.” | T: has bewildered, I tell you, Death’s king.
sn35.229:4.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn35.230
sn35.230:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, suppose an angler was to cast a baited hook into a deep lake. | T: “Monks, just as if a fisherman were to cast a baited hook into a deep lake and a fish with its eye out for food would swallow it—
sn35.230:1.2 | S: Seeing the bait, a fish would swallow it. | T: (none)
sn35.230:1.3 | S: And so the fish that swallowed the hook would meet with tragedy and disaster, and the angler can do what he wants with it. | T: so that the fish that had thus swallowed the fisherman’s hook would fall into misfortune & disaster, and the fisherman could do with it as he will—
sn35.230:2.1 | S: In the same way, there are these six hooks in the world that mean tragedy and slaughter for living creatures. | T: in the same way, there are these six hooks in the world for the misfortune of beings, for the slaughter of those that breathe.
sn35.230:2.2 | S: What six? | T: Which six?
sn35.230:2.3 | S: There are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are forms, monks, cognizable via the eye—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire.
sn35.230:2.4 | S: If a mendicant approves, welcomes, and keeps clinging to them, | T: If a monk relishes them, welcomes them, & remains fastened to them,
sn35.230:2.5 | S: they’re called a mendicant who has swallowed Māra’s hook. They’ve met with tragedy and disaster, and the Wicked One can do with them what he wants. | T: he is said to be a monk who has swallowed Māra’s hook, who has fallen into misfortune & disaster. The Evil One can do with him as he will.
sn35.230:2.6 | S: There are sounds … smells … tastes … touches … | T: “There are sounds cognizable via the ear… “There are aromas cognizable via the nose… “There are flavors cognizable via the tongue… “There are tactile sensations cognizable via the body…
sn35.230:3.1 | S: ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are ideas cognizable via the intellect—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire.
sn35.230:3.2 | S: If a mendicant approves, welcomes, and keeps clinging to them, | T: If a monk relishes them, welcomes them, & remains fastened to them,
sn35.230:3.3 | S: they’re called a mendicant who has swallowed Māra’s hook. They’ve met with tragedy and disaster, and the Wicked One can do with them what he wants. | T: he is said to be a monk who has swallowed Māra’s hook, who has fallen into misfortune & disaster. The Evil One can do with him as he will.
sn35.230:4.1 | S: There are sights known by the eye, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “Now, there are forms cognizable via the eye—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire.
sn35.230:4.2 | S: If a mendicant doesn’t approve, welcome, and keep clinging to them, they’re called a mendicant who hasn’t swallowed Māra’s hook. They’ve broken the hook, destroyed it. They haven’t met with tragedy and disaster, and the Wicked One cannot do with them what he wants. | T: If a monk does not relish them, welcome them, or remain fastened to them, he is said to be a monk who has not swallowed Māra’s hook, who has snapped the hook, who has broken the hook, who has not fallen into misfortune & disaster. The Evil One cannot do with him as he will.
sn35.230:5.1 | S: There are sounds … smells … tastes … touches … | T: “There are sounds cognizable via the ear… “There are aromas cognizable via the nose… “There are flavors cognizable via the tongue… “There are tactile sensations cognizable via the body…
sn35.230:5.2 | S: ideas known by the mind, which are likable, desirable, agreeable, pleasant, sensual, and arousing. | T: “There are ideas cognizable via the intellect—agreeable, pleasing, charming, endearing, enticing, linked to sensual desire.
sn35.230:5.3 | S: If a mendicant doesn’t approve, welcome, and keep clinging to them, they’re called a mendicant who hasn’t swallowed Māra’s hook. They’ve broken the hook, destroyed it. They haven’t met with tragedy and disaster, and the Wicked One cannot do with them what he wants.” | T: If a monk does not relish them, welcome them, or remain fastened to them, he is said to be a monk who has not swallowed Māra’s hook, who has snapped the hook, who has broken the hook, who has not fallen into misfortune & disaster. The Evil One cannot do with him as he will.”
sn35.230:5.4 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn42.10
sn42.10:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha at the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
sn42.10:1.2 | S: Now at that time while the king’s retinue was sitting together in the royal compound this discussion came up among them, | T: Now at that time, when the king’s assembly had gathered and was sitting together in the royal palace, this topic of conversation arose:
sn42.10:1.3 | S: “Gold and currency are proper for ascetics who follow the Sakyan. They accept and receive gold and currency.” | T: “Money [lit: gold & silver] is allowable for the Sakyan-son contemplatives. The Sakyan-son contemplatives consent to money. The Sakyan-son contemplatives accept money.”
sn42.10:2.1 | S: Now at that time the chief Maṇicūḷaka was sitting in that assembly. | T: At that time Maṇicūḷaka the headman was sitting in that assembly,
sn42.10:2.2 | S: He said to that retinue, | T: so he said to them,
sn42.10:2.3 | S: “Good masters, don’t say that. | T: “Don’t say that, masters.
sn42.10:2.4 | S: Gold and currency are not proper for ascetics who follow the Sakyan. They neither accept nor receive gold and currency. They have set aside gems and gold, and quit using gold and currency.” | T: Money is not allowable for the Sakyan-son contemplatives. The Sakyan-son contemplatives do not consent to money. The Sakyan-son contemplatives do not accept money. The Sakyan-son contemplatives have given up gold & jewelry, have renounced money.”
sn42.10:2.5 | S: He was able to persuade that assembly. | T: And he was able to convince the assembly.
sn42.10:2.6 | S: Then Maṇicūḷaka went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and told him what had happened. He then said, | T: Then he went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One,
sn42.10:2.7 | P: “idha, bhante, rājantepure rājaparisāya sannisinnānaṁ sannipatitānaṁ ayamantarākathā udapādi: | T: “Just now, lord, when the king’s assembly had gathered and was sitting together in the royal palace, this topic of conversation arose:
sn42.10:2.8 | P: ‘kappati samaṇānaṁ sakyaputtiyānaṁ jātarūparajataṁ, sādiyanti samaṇā sakyaputtiyā jātarūparajataṁ, paṭiggaṇhanti samaṇā sakyaputtiyā jātarūparajatan’ti. | T: ‘Money is allowable for the Sakyan-son contemplatives. The Sakyan-son contemplatives consent to money. The Sakyan-son contemplatives accept money.’
sn42.10:2.9 | P: Evaṁ vutte, ahaṁ, bhante, taṁ parisaṁ etadavocaṁ: | T: When this was said, I said to them,
sn42.10:2.10 | P: ‘mā ayyo evaṁ avacuttha. | T: ‘Don’t say that, masters.
sn42.10:2.11 | P: Na kappati samaṇānaṁ sakyaputtiyānaṁ jātarūparajataṁ, na sādiyanti samaṇā sakyaputtiyā jātarūparajataṁ, nappaṭiggaṇhanti samaṇā sakyaputtiyā jātarūparajataṁ, nikkhittamaṇisuvaṇṇā samaṇā sakyaputtiyā apetajātarūparajatā’ti. | T: Money is not allowable for the Sakyan-son contemplatives. The Sakyan-son contemplatives do not consent to money. The Sakyan-son contemplatives do not accept money. The Sakyan-son contemplatives have given up gold & jewelry, have renounced money.’
sn42.10:2.12 | P: Asakkhiṁ khvāhaṁ, bhante, taṁ parisaṁ saññāpetuṁ. | T: And I was able to convince the assembly.
sn42.10:2.13 | S: “Answering this way, I trust that I repeat what the Buddha has said, and don’t misrepresent him with an untruth. I trust my explanation is in line with the teaching, and that there are no legitimate grounds for rebuttal or criticism.” | T: Answering in this way, lord, am I speaking in line with what the Blessed One has said, am I not misrepresenting the Blessed One with what is unfactual, am I answering in line with the Dhamma so that no one whose thinking is in line with the Dhamma will have grounds for criticizing me?”
sn42.10:3.1 | S: “Indeed, in answering this way you repeat what I’ve said, and don’t misrepresent me with an untruth. Your explanation is in line with the teaching, and there are no legitimate grounds for rebuttal or criticism. | T: “Yes, headman, in answering in this way you are speaking in line with what I have said, you are not misrepresenting me with what is unfactual, and you are answering in line with the Dhamma so that no one whose thinking is in line with the Dhamma will have grounds for criticizing you.
sn42.10:3.2 | S: Gold and currency are not proper for ascetics who follow the Sakyan. They neither accept nor receive gold and currency. They have set aside gems and gold, and quit using gold and currency. | T: For money is not allowable for the Sakyan-son contemplatives, the Sakyan-son contemplatives do not consent to money, the Sakyan-son contemplatives do not accept money, the Sakyan-son contemplatives have given up gold & jewelry, have renounced money.
sn42.10:3.3 | S: If gold and currency were proper for them, then the five kinds of sensual stimulation would also be proper. | T: For anyone for whom money is allowable, the five strings of sensuality are also allowable.
sn42.10:3.4 | S: And if the five kinds of sensual stimulation are proper for them, you should categorically regard them as not having the qualities of an ascetic or a follower of the Sakyan. | T: For anyone for whom the five strings of sensuality are allowable, money is allowable. That you can unequivocally recognize as not the quality of a contemplative, not the quality of a Sakyan son.
sn42.10:3.5 | S: Rather, chief, I say this: | T: “Now I do say that thatch may be sought for by one needing thatch, wood may be sought for by one needing wood, a cart may be sought for by one needing a cart, a workman may be sought for by one needing a workman,
sn42.10:3.6 | S: Straw may be looked for by one needing straw; wood may be looked for by one needing wood; a cart may be looked for by one needing a cart; a workman may be looked for by one needing a workman. | T: (none)
sn42.10:3.7 | S: But in any case, there is no way they can accept or look for gold and currency, I say.” | T: but by no means do I say that money may be consented to or sought for in any way at all.”
sn42.10:3.8 | P: Dasamaṁ. | T: (none)

## sn6.2
sn6.2:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that
sn6.2:1.2 | S: At one time, when he was first awakened, the Buddha was staying in Uruvelā at the root of the goatherd’s banyan tree on the bank of the Nerañjarā River. | T: on one occasion, when the Blessed One was newly self-awakened, he was staying near Uruvelā on the bank of the Nerañjarā River, at the foot of the Goatherd’s Banyan Tree.
sn6.2:1.3 | S: Then as he was in private retreat this thought came to his mind, | T: Then, while he was alone & in seclusion, this line of thinking arose in his awareness:
sn6.2:1.4 | S: “One without respect and reverence lives in suffering. What ascetic or brahmin should I honor and respect and rely on?” | T: “One suffers if dwelling without reverence or deference. Now on what contemplative or brahman can I dwell in dependence, honoring & respecting him?”
sn6.2:2.1 | S: Then it occurred to him: | T: Then the thought occurred to him:
sn6.2:2.2 | S: “I’d honor and respect and rely on another ascetic or brahmin so as to complete the entire spectrum of ethics, if it were incomplete. | T: “It would be for the sake of perfecting an unperfected aggregate of virtue that I would dwell in dependence on another contemplative or brahman, honoring & respecting him.
sn6.2:2.3 | S: But I don’t see any other ascetic or brahmin in this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—who is more accomplished than myself in ethics, who I should honor and respect and rely on. | T: However, in this world with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, I do not see another contemplative or brahman more consummate in virtue than I, on whom I could dwell in dependence, honoring & respecting him.
sn6.2:3.1 | S: I’d honor and respect and rely on another ascetic or brahmin so as to complete the entire spectrum of immersion, if it were incomplete. | T: “It would be for the sake of perfecting an unperfected aggregate of concentration that I would dwell in dependence on another contemplative or brahman, honoring & respecting him.
sn6.2:3.2 | S: But I don’t see any other ascetic or brahmin … who is more accomplished than myself in immersion … | T: However, in this world with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, I do not see another contemplative or brahman more consummate in concentration than I, on whom I could dwell in dependence, honoring & respecting him.
sn6.2:4.1 | S: I’d honor and respect and rely on another ascetic or brahmin so as to complete the entire spectrum of wisdom, if it were incomplete. | T: “It would be for the sake of perfecting an unperfected aggregate of discernment that I would dwell in dependence on another contemplative or brahman, honoring & respecting him.
sn6.2:4.2 | S: But I don’t see any other ascetic or brahmin … who is more accomplished than myself in wisdom … | T: However, in this world with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, I do not see another contemplative or brahman more consummate in discernment than I, on whom I could dwell in dependence, honoring & respecting him.
sn6.2:5.1 | S: I’d honor and respect and rely on another ascetic or brahmin so as to complete the entire spectrum of freedom, if it were incomplete. | T: “It would be for the sake of perfecting an unperfected aggregate of release that I would dwell in dependence on another contemplative or brahman, honoring & respecting him.
sn6.2:5.2 | S: But I don’t see any other ascetic or brahmin … who is more accomplished than myself in freedom … | T: However, in this world with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, I do not see another contemplative or brahman more consummate in release than I, on whom I could dwell in dependence, honoring & respecting him.
sn6.2:6.1 | S: I’d honor and respect and rely on another ascetic or brahmin so as to complete the entire spectrum of the knowledge and vision of freedom, if it were incomplete. | T: “It would be for the sake of perfecting an unperfected aggregate of knowledge & vision of release that I would dwell in dependence on another contemplative or brahman, honoring & respecting him.
sn6.2:6.2 | S: But I don’t see any other ascetic or brahmin in this world—with its gods, Māras, and divinities, this population with its ascetics and brahmins, its gods and humans—who is more accomplished than myself in the knowledge and vision of freedom, who I should honor and respect and rely on. | T: However, in this world with its devas, Māras, & Brahmās, in this generation with its contemplatives & brahmans, its royalty & commonfolk, I do not see another contemplative or brahman more consummate in knowledge & vision of release than I, on whom I could dwell in dependence, honoring & respecting him.
sn6.2:6.3 | S: Why don’t I honor and respect and rely on the same Dhamma to which I was awakened?” | T: “What if I were to dwell in dependence on this very Dhamma to which I have fully awakened, honoring & respecting it?”
sn6.2:7.1 | S: Then the divinity Sahampati knew the Buddha’s train of thought. As easily as a strong person would extend or contract their arm, he vanished from the realm of divinity and reappeared in front of the Buddha. | T: Then, having known with his own awareness the line of thinking in the Blessed One’s awareness—just as a strong man might extend his flexed arm or flex his extended arm—Brahmā Sahampati disappeared from the Brahmā world and reappeared in front of the Blessed One.
sn6.2:7.2 | S: He arranged his robe over one shoulder, raised his cupped palms toward the Buddha, and said: | T: Arranging his upper robe over one shoulder, he saluted the Blessed One with his hands before his heart and said to him:
sn6.2:7.3 | S: “That’s so true, Blessed One! That’s so true, Holy One! | T: “So it is, Blessed One! So it is, One-Well-Gone!
sn6.2:7.4 | S: All the perfected ones, the fully awakened Buddhas who lived in the past honored and respected and relied on this same teaching. | T: Those who were arahants, Rightly Self-awakened Ones in the past—they, too, dwelled in dependence on the very Dhamma itself, honoring & respecting it.
sn6.2:7.5 | S: All the perfected ones, the fully awakened Buddhas who will live in the future will honor and respect and rely on this same teaching. | T: Those who will be arahants, Rightly Self-awakened Ones in the future—they, too, will dwell in dependence on the very Dhamma itself, honoring & respecting it.
sn6.2:7.6 | S: May the Blessed One, who is the perfected one, the fully awakened Buddha at present, also honor and respect and rely on this same teaching.” | T: And let the Blessed One, who is at present the arahant, the Rightly Self-awakened One, dwell in dependence on the very Dhamma itself, honoring & respecting it.”
sn6.2:7.7 | S: That is what the divinity Sahampati said. Then he went on to say: | T: That is what Brahmā Sahampati said. Having said that, he further said this:
sn6.2:8.1 | S: “All Buddhas in the past, | T: “Past Buddhas,
sn6.2:8.2 | S: the Buddhas of the future, | T: future Buddhas,
sn6.2:8.3 | S: and the Buddha at present— | T: & he who is the Buddha now,
sn6.2:8.4 | S: destroyer of the sorrows of many— | T: removing the sorrow of many—
sn6.2:9.1 | S: respecting the true teaching | T: all have dwelt, will dwell, he dwells, revering the true Dhamma.
sn6.2:9.2 | S: they did live, they do live, | T: (none)
sn6.2:9.3 | S: and they also will live. | T: (none)
sn6.2:9.4 | S: This is the nature of the Buddhas. | T: This, for Buddhas, is a natural law.
sn6.2:10.1 | S: Therefore someone who desires self-knowledge, | T: Therefore one who desires his own good,
sn6.2:10.2 | S: and aspires to greatness themselves, | T: aspiring for greatness,
sn6.2:10.3 | S: should respect the true teaching, | T: should respect the true Dhamma,
sn6.2:10.4 | S: remembering the instructions of the Buddhas.” | T: recollecting the Buddhas’ Teaching.”


