## an3.51
an3.51:1.1 | S: Then two old brahmins—elderly and senior, who were advanced in years and had reached the final stage of life, a hundred and twenty years old—went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. Then two brahmans—feeble old men, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life, 120 years old—went to the Blessed One. On arrival, they exchanged courteous greetings with him and,
an3.51:1.2 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, they sat down to one side, and said to the Buddha: | T: after an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, sat to one side. As they were sitting there, they said to him:
an3.51:1.3 | S: “We brahmins, worthy Gotama, are old, elderly and senior, we’re advanced in years and have reached the final stage of life; we’re a hundred and twenty years old. | T: “Master Gotama, we are brahmans—feeble old men, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life, 120 years old.
an3.51:1.4 | S: And we haven’t done what is good and skillful, nor have we made a shelter from fear. | T: And we have done no admirable deeds, no skillful deeds, no deeds that allay our fears.
an3.51:1.5 | S: Advise us, worthy Gotama, instruct us! It will be for our lasting welfare and happiness.” | T: Teach us, Master Gotama. Instruct us, Master Gotama, for our long-term benefit & happiness.”
an3.51:2.1 | S: “Indeed, brahmins, you’re old, elderly and senior. | T: “Indeed, brahmans, you are feeble old men, aged, advanced in years, having come to the last stage of life, 120 years old.
an3.51:2.2 | S: And you haven’t done what is good and skillful, nor have you made a shelter from fear. | T: And you have done no admirable deeds, no skillful deeds, no deeds that allay your fears.
an3.51:2.3 | S: This world is led onward by old age, sickness, and death. | T: This world is swept away by aging, by illness, by death. With the world thus swept away by aging, illness, & death,
an3.51:2.4 | S: But restraint here by way of body, speech, and mind is the shelter, asylum, island, refuge, and final haven for the departed. | T: any restraint of body, speech, & intellect practiced here will be one’s shelter, cave, island, & refuge after death in the world beyond.”
an3.51:3.1 | S: This life, so very short, is led onward. | T: It’s swept along: life, its next-to-nothing span.
an3.51:3.2 | S: One led on to old age has no shelter. | T: For one swept on by aging no shelters exist.
an3.51:3.3 | S: Seeing this peril in death, | T: Keeping sight of this danger in death,
an3.51:3.4 | S: do good deeds that bring you joy. | T: do merit-deeds that bring bliss.
an3.51:4.1 | S: The restraint practiced here— | T: When you’re restrained here
an3.51:4.2 | S: of body, speech, and mind— | T: in body, speech, & awareness;
an3.51:4.3 | S: leads the departed to happiness, | T: (none)
an3.51:4.4 | S: as the good deeds done while living.” | T: when you make merit while still alive: That will be for your bliss after death.
an3.51:4.5 | P: Paṭhamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.183
an4.183:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Grove, the squirrels’ feeding ground. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha in the Bamboo Forest, the Squirrels’ Sanctuary.
an4.183:1.2 | S: Then Vassakāra the brahmin, a chief minister of Magadha, went up to the Buddha, and exchanged greetings with him. | T: Then Vassakāra the brahman, the minister to the king of Magadha, approached the Blessed One and, on arrival, exchanged courteous greetings with him.
an4.183:1.3 | S: When the greetings and polite conversation were over, he sat down to one side and said to the Buddha: | T: After an exchange of friendly greetings & courtesies, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an4.183:2.1 | S: “Worthy Gotama, this is my doctrine and view: | T: “I am of the view, of the opinion,
an4.183:2.2 | S: There’s nothing wrong with talking about what you’ve seen, saying: | T: that when anyone speaks of what he has seen, (saying,)
an4.183:2.3 | S: ‘So I have seen.’ | T: ‘Thus have I seen,’ there is no fault in that.
an4.183:2.4 | S: There’s nothing wrong with talking about what you’ve heard, saying: | T: When anyone speaks of what he has heard, (saying,)
an4.183:2.5 | S: ‘So I have heard.’ | T: ‘Thus have I heard,’ there is no fault in that.
an4.183:2.6 | S: There’s nothing wrong with talking about what you’ve thought, saying: | T: When anyone speaks of what he has sensed, (saying,)
an4.183:2.7 | S: ‘So I have thought.’ | T: ‘Thus have I sensed,’ there is no fault in that.
an4.183:2.8 | S: There’s nothing wrong with talking about what you’ve known, saying: | T: When anyone speaks of what he has cognized, (saying,)
an4.183:2.9 | S: ‘So I have known.’” | T: ‘Thus have I cognized,’ there is no fault in that.”
an4.183:3.1 | S: “Brahmin, I don’t say you should talk about everything you see, hear, think, and know. | T: (The Blessed One responded:) “I do not say, brahman, that everything that has been seen should be spoken about. Nor do I say that everything that has been seen should not be spoken about. I do not say that everything that has been heard… everything that has been sensed… everything that has been cognized should be spoken about. Nor do I say that everything that has been cognized should not be spoken about.
an4.183:3.2 | S: But I also don’t say you should talk about nothing you see, hear, think, and know. | T: (none)
an4.183:3.3 | P: nāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ sutaṁ bhāsitabbanti vadāmi; | T: (none)
an4.183:3.4 | P: na panāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ sutaṁ na bhāsitabbanti vadāmi; | T: (none)
an4.183:3.5 | P: nāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ mutaṁ bhāsitabbanti vadāmi; | T: (none)
an4.183:3.6 | P: na panāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ mutaṁ na bhāsitabbanti vadāmi; | T: (none)
an4.183:3.7 | P: nāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ viññātaṁ bhāsitabbanti vadāmi; | T: (none)
an4.183:3.8 | P: na panāhaṁ, brāhmaṇa, sabbaṁ viññātaṁ na bhāsitabbanti vadāmi. | T: (none)
an4.183:4.1 | S: When talking about certain things you’ve seen, heard, thought, or known, unskillful qualities grow while skillful qualities decline. I say that you shouldn’t talk about those things. | T: “When, for one who speaks of what has been seen, unskillful qualities increase and skillful qualities decrease, then that sort of thing should not be spoken about. But when, for one who speaks of what has been seen, unskillful qualities decrease and skillful qualities increase, then that sort of thing should be spoken about.
an4.183:4.2 | S: When talking about other things you’ve seen, heard, thought, or known, unskillful qualities decline while skillful qualities grow. I say that you should talk about those things.” | T: “When, for one who speaks of what has been heard… what has been sensed… what has been cognized, unskillful qualities increase and skillful qualities decrease, then that sort of thing should not be spoken about. But when, for one who speaks of what has been cognized, unskillful qualities decrease and skillful qualities increase, then that sort of thing should be spoken about.”
an4.183:5.1 | P: Yañhi, brāhmaṇa, sutaṁ bhāsato akusalā dhammā abhivaḍḍhanti, kusalā dhammā parihāyanti, evarūpaṁ sutaṁ na bhāsitabbanti vadāmi. | T: (none)
an4.183:5.2 | P: Yañca khvassa, brāhmaṇa, sutaṁ abhāsato kusalā dhammā parihāyanti, akusalā dhammā abhivaḍḍhanti, evarūpaṁ sutaṁ bhāsitabbanti vadāmi. | T: (none)
an4.183:6.1 | P: Yañhi, brāhmaṇa, mutaṁ bhāsato akusalā dhammā abhivaḍḍhanti, kusalā dhammā parihāyanti, evarūpaṁ mutaṁ na bhāsitabbanti vadāmi. | T: (none)
an4.183:6.2 | P: Yañca khvassa, brāhmaṇa, mutaṁ abhāsato kusalā dhammā parihāyanti, akusalā dhammā abhivaḍḍhanti, evarūpaṁ mutaṁ bhāsitabbanti vadāmi. | T: (none)
an4.183:7.1 | P: Yañhi, brāhmaṇa, viññātaṁ bhāsato akusalā dhammā abhivaḍḍhanti, kusalā dhammā parihāyanti, evarūpaṁ viññātaṁ na bhāsitabbanti vadāmi. | T: (none)
an4.183:7.2 | P: Yañca khvassa, brāhmaṇa, viññātaṁ abhāsato kusalā dhammā parihāyanti, akusalā dhammā abhivaḍḍhanti, evarūpaṁ viññātaṁ bhāsitabbanti vadāmī”ti. | T: (none)
an4.183:8.1 | S: Then Vassakāra the brahmin, having approved and agreed with what the Buddha said, rose from his seat and left. | T: Then Vassakāra the brahman, delighting & rejoicing in the Blessed One’s words, got up from his seat and left.
an4.183:8.2 | P: Tatiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.36
an4.36:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was traveling along the road between Ukkaṭṭhā and Setavyā, | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was traveling along the road between Ukkaṭṭha and Setabya, and Doṇa the brahman was also traveling along the road between Ukkaṭṭha and Setabya.
an4.36:1.2 | S: as was the brahmin Doṇa. | T: (none)
an4.36:1.3 | S: Doṇa saw that the Buddha’s footprints had thousand-spoked wheels, with rims and hubs, complete in every detail. | T: Doṇa the brahman saw, in the Blessed One’s footprints, wheels with 1,000 spokes, together with rims and hubs, complete in all their features.
an4.36:1.4 | S: It occurred to him, | T: On seeing them, the thought occurred to him,
an4.36:1.5 | S: “Oh lord, how incredible, how amazing! | T: “How amazing! How astounding!
an4.36:1.6 | S: Surely these couldn’t be the footprints of a human being?” | T: These are not the footprints of a human being!”
an4.36:1.7 | S: The Buddha had left the road and sat at the root of a tree cross-legged, setting his body straight, and bringing mindfulness to the present. | T: Then the Blessed One, leaving the road, went to sit at the root of a certain tree—his legs crossed, his body erect, with mindfulness established to the fore.
an4.36:1.8 | S: Then Doṇa, following the Buddha’s footprints, saw him sitting at the tree root—impressive and inspiring, with peaceful faculties and heart, attained to the highest self-control and serenity, like an elephant with tamed, guarded, and controlled faculties. | T: Then Doṇa, following the Blessed One’s footprints, saw him sitting at the root of the tree: confident, inspiring confidence, his senses calmed, his mind calmed, having attained the utmost control & tranquility, tamed, guarded, his senses restrained, a nāga.
an4.36:1.9 | S: He went up to the Buddha and said to him: | T: On seeing him, he went to him and said,
an4.36:2.1 | S: “Sir, might you be a god?” | T: “Master, are you a deva?”
an4.36:2.2 | S: “I will not be a god, brahmin.” | T: “No, brahman, I am not a deva.”
an4.36:2.3 | S: “Might you be a centaur?” | T: “Are you a gandhabba?”
an4.36:2.4 | S: “I will not be a centaur.” | T: “No….”
an4.36:2.5 | S: “Might you be a spirit?” | T: “… a yakkha?”
an4.36:2.6 | S: “I will not be a spirit.” | T: “No….”
an4.36:2.7 | S: “Might you be a human?” | T: “… a human being?”
an4.36:2.8 | S: “I will not be a human.” | T: “No, brahman, I am not a human being.”
an4.36:3.1 | S: “When asked whether you might be a god, centaur, spirit, or human, | T: “When asked, ‘Are you a deva?’
an4.36:3.2 | S: you answer that you will not be any of these. | T: you answer, ‘No, brahman, I am not a deva.’
an4.36:3.3 | P: ‘Gandhabbo no bhavaṁ bhavissatī’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno: | T: When asked, ‘Are you a gandhabba?’
an4.36:3.4 | P: ‘na kho ahaṁ, brāhmaṇa, gandhabbo bhavissāmī’ti vadesi. | T: you answer, ‘No, brahman, I am not a gandhabba.’
an4.36:3.5 | P: ‘Yakkho no bhavaṁ bhavissatī’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno: | T: When asked, ‘Are you a yakkha?’
an4.36:3.6 | P: ‘na kho ahaṁ, brāhmaṇa, yakkho bhavissāmī’ti vadesi. | T: you answer, ‘No, brahman, I am not a yakkha.’
an4.36:3.7 | P: ‘Manusso no bhavaṁ bhavissatī’ti, iti puṭṭho samāno: | T: When asked, ‘Are you a human being?’
an4.36:3.8 | P: ‘na kho ahaṁ, brāhmaṇa, manusso bhavissāmī’ti vadesi. | T: you answer, ‘No, brahman, I am not a human being.’
an4.36:3.9 | S: So what then might you be?” | T: Then what sort of being are you?”
an4.36:4.1 | S: “Brahmin, if I had not given up defilements I might have become a god … a centaur … a spirit … or a human. But I have given up those defilements, cut them off at the root, made them like a palm stump, obliterated them so they are unable to arise in the future. | T: “Brahman, the effluents by which—if they were not abandoned—I would be a deva: Those are abandoned by me, their root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising.
an4.36:4.2 | P: Yesaṁ kho ahaṁ, brāhmaṇa, āsavānaṁ appahīnattā gandhabbo bhaveyyaṁ … yakkho bhaveyyaṁ … manusso bhaveyyaṁ, te me āsavā pahīnā ucchinnamūlā tālāvatthukatā anabhāvaṅkatā āyatiṁ anuppādadhammā. | T: The effluents by which—if they were not abandoned—I would be a gandhabba… a yakkha… a human being: Those are abandoned by me, their root destroyed, made like a palmyra stump, deprived of the conditions of development, not destined for future arising.
an4.36:4.3 | S: Suppose there was a blue water lily, or a pink or white lotus. Though it sprouted and grew in the water, it would rise up above the water and stand with no water clinging to it. | T: “Just like a red, blue, or white lotus—born in the water, grown in the water, rising up above the water—stands unsmeared by the water,
an4.36:4.4 | S: In the same way, though I was born and grew up in the world, I live having mastered the world, unsullied by the world. | T: in the same way I—born in the world, grown in the world, having overcome the world—live unsmeared by the world.
an4.36:4.5 | S: Remember me, brahmin, as a Buddha. | T: Remember me, brahman, as ‘awakened.’
an4.36:5.1 | S: I could have been reborn as a god, | T: “The effluents by which I would go to a deva-state,
an4.36:5.2 | S: or as a centaur flying through the sky. | T: or become a gandhabba in the sky,
an4.36:5.3 | S: I could have become a spirit, | T: or go to a yakkha-state & human-state:
an4.36:5.4 | S: or returned as a human. | T: (none)
an4.36:5.5 | S: But I’ve ended those defilements, | T: Those have been destroyed by me,
an4.36:5.6 | S: they’re blown away and mown down. | T: ruined, their stems removed.
an4.36:6.1 | S: Like a graceful lotus, | T: Like a blue lotus, rising up,
an4.36:6.2 | S: to which water does not cling, | T: unsmeared by water,
an4.36:6.3 | S: the world doesn’t cling to me, | T: unsmeared am I by the world,
an4.36:6.4 | S: and so, brahmin, I am a Buddha.” | T: and so, brahman, I’m awake.”
an4.36:6.5 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.94
an4.94:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, these four individuals are found in the world. | T: “Monks, these four types of individuals are to be found existing in world.
an4.94:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.94:1.3 | S: One individual has internal serenity of heart, but not the higher wisdom of discernment of principles. | T: “There is the case of the individual who has attained internal tranquility of awareness, but not insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:1.4 | S: One individual has the higher wisdom of discernment of principles, but not internal serenity of heart. | T: Then there is the case of the individual who has attained insight into phenomena through heightened discernment, but not internal tranquility of awareness.
an4.94:1.5 | S: One individual has neither internal serenity of heart, nor the higher wisdom of discernment of principles. | T: Then there is the case of the individual who has attained neither internal tranquility of awareness nor insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:1.6 | S: One individual has both internal serenity of heart, and the higher wisdom of discernment of principles. | T: And then there is the case of the individual who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:2.1 | S: As for the individual who has serenity but not discernment: they should approach someone who has discernment and ask: | T: “The individual who has attained internal tranquility of awareness, but not insight into phenomena through heightened discernment, should approach an individual who has attained insight into phenomena through heightened discernment and ask him:
an4.94:2.2 | S: ‘Reverend, how should conditions be seen? | T: ‘How should fabrications be regarded? How should they be investigated? How should they be seen with insight?’
an4.94:2.3 | S: How should they be comprehended? | T: (none)
an4.94:2.4 | S: How should they be discerned?’ | T: (none)
an4.94:2.5 | S: They would answer from their own experience: | T: The other will answer in line with what he has seen & experienced:
an4.94:2.6 | S: ‘This is how conditions should be seen, comprehended, and discerned.’ | T: ‘Fabrications should be regarded in this way. Fabrications should be investigated in this way. Fabrications should be seen in this way with insight.’
an4.94:2.7 | S: After some time they have both serenity and discernment. | T: Then eventually he [the first] will become one who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:3.1 | S: As for the individual who has discernment but not serenity: they should approach someone who has serenity and ask: | T: “As for the individual who has attained insight into phenomena through heightened discernment, but not internal tranquility of awareness, he should approach an individual who has attained internal tranquility of awareness… and ask him:
an4.94:3.2 | S: ‘Reverend, how should the mind be stilled? | T: ‘How should the mind be steadied?
an4.94:3.3 | S: How should it be settled? | T: How should it be made to settle down?
an4.94:3.4 | S: How should it be unified? | T: How should it be unified?
an4.94:3.5 | S: How should it be immersed in samādhi?’ | T: How should it be concentrated?’
an4.94:3.6 | S: They would answer from their own experience: | T: The other will answer in line with what he has seen & experienced:
an4.94:3.7 | S: ‘Reverend, this is how the mind should be stilled, settled, unified, and immersed in samādhi.’ | T: ‘The mind should be steadied in this way. The mind should be made to settle down in this way. The mind should be unified in this way. The mind should be concentrated in this way.’
an4.94:3.8 | S: After some time they have both discernment and serenity. | T: Then eventually he [the first] will become one who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:4.1 | S: As for the individual who has neither serenity nor discernment: they should approach someone who has serenity and discernment and ask: | T: “As for the individual who has attained neither internal tranquility of awareness nor insight into phenomena through heightened discernment, he should approach an individual who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment… and ask him:
an4.94:4.2 | S: ‘Reverend, how should the mind be stilled? | T: ‘How should the mind be steadied?
an4.94:4.3 | S: How should it be settled? | T: How should it be made to settle down?
an4.94:4.4 | S: How should it be unified? | T: How should it be unified?
an4.94:4.5 | S: How should it be immersed in samādhi?’ | T: How should it be concentrated?
an4.94:4.6 | S: How should conditions be seen? | T: How should fabrications be regarded?
an4.94:4.7 | S: How should they be comprehended? | T: How should they be investigated?
an4.94:4.8 | S: How should they be discerned?’ | T: How should they be seen with insight?’
an4.94:4.9 | S: They would answer as they’ve seen and known: | T: The other will answer in line with what he has seen & experienced:
an4.94:4.10 | S: ‘Reverend, this is how the mind should be stilled, settled, unified, and immersed in samādhi. And this is how conditions should be seen, comprehended, and discerned.’ | T: ‘The mind should be steadied in this way. The mind should be made to settle down in this way. The mind should be unified in this way. The mind should be concentrated in this way. Fabrications should be regarded in this way. Fabrications should be investigated in this way. Fabrications should be seen in this way with insight.’
an4.94:4.11 | S: After some time they have both serenity and discernment. | T: Then eventually he [the first] will become one who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment.
an4.94:5.1 | S: As for the individual who has both serenity and discernment: grounded on those skillful qualities, they should practice meditation further to end the defilements. | T: “As for the individual who has attained both internal tranquility of awareness & insight into phenomena through heightened discernment, his duty is to make an effort in establishing [‘tuning’] those very same skillful qualities to a higher degree for the ending of the effluents.
an4.94:5.2 | S: These are the four individuals found in the world.” | T: “These are four types of individuals to be found existing in world.”
an4.94:5.3 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp1.1
snp1.1:1.1 | S: When anger surges, they drive it out, | T: The monk who subdues his arisen anger
snp1.1:1.2 | S: as with medicine a cobra’s spreading venom. | T: as, with herbs, snake-venom once it has spread,
snp1.1:1.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:1.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:2.1 | S: They’ve cut off greed entirely, | T: The monk who has cut off passion without leaving a trace,
snp1.1:2.2 | S: like a lotus plucked flower and stalk. | T: as he would, plunging into a lake, a lotus,
snp1.1:2.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—
snp1.1:2.4 | S: as a serpent its old worn-out skin. | T: as a snake, its decrepit old skin.
snp1.1:3.1 | S: They’ve cut off craving entirely, | T: The monk who has cut off craving without leaving a trace,
snp1.1:3.2 | S: drying up that swift-flowing stream. | T: drying up the swift-flowing flood,
snp1.1:3.3 | S: Such a mendicant sheds the near shore and the far, | T: sloughs off the near shore & far—

